     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · India Today · 2026-10-03 · editor 8/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>PM Modi Commends Pilot's Bravery Amid Nationwide Protests on October 2</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#National News</span> <span>#Protests</span> <span>#Civil Aviation</span></div>
       <p>Prime Minister Narendra Modi recently spoke with Captain Smit Machhar, the Indian pilot hailed for his heroism following a severe mid-air crisis aboard a Flydubai Boeing 737 MAX 8 flight from Dubai to Tel Aviv. At 34,000 feet, Captain Machhar was attacked by his Omani co-pilot, causing the aircraft to plunge over 1,400 feet within 30 seconds. Despite sustaining serious wounds, the captain, drawing on 17 years of flying experience and reciting the Hanuman Chalisa for strength, managed to unlock the cockpit door, allowing crew and passengers to intervene and secure the plane. Speaking from the hospital, Captain Machhar recounted the ordeal, with PM Modi commending his quick decision-making and composure in prioritizing passenger safety.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-03/pm-modi-commends-pilots-bravery-amid-nationwide-protests-on-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Concurrently on October 2, mass demonstrations erupted across the national capital, with the INDIA bloc, student outfits, and civil society groups demanding the resignation of Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar. Protesters, including members from Congress, Aam Aadmi Party, Left parties, and Rashtriya Janata Dal, attempted to gather at Jantar Mantar, where Section 163 was imposed. Delhi Police responded by detaining over 1,100 demonstrators, including senior Left leaders MA Baby and Brinda Karat, and transporting them away in buses. The opposition accused the Election Commission of removing eligible voters from electoral rolls, a claim the central government defended while dismissing the agitation.</p><p>In Mumbai, coinciding with Gandhi Jayanti, Shivaji Park became the focal point for multiple gatherings. The Citizens for Justice and Peace (CJP) organized a protest drawing youth participation and public figures like music composer Vishal Dadlani, Abhijeet Dipke, Saurabh Das, and Ashutosh Ranka. Demonstrators voiced demands in defense of constitutional values, democratic rights, and the legacies of Mahatma Gandhi, Dr. B.R. Ambedkar, and Jawaharlal Nehru, invoking Gandhi's principles of peaceful resistance. They also criticized government policies, administrative functioning, demonetisation, and public management during the COVID-19 pandemic.</p><p>Furthermore, two opposing demonstrations unfolded simultaneously at Shivaji Park, with a counter-protest emerging to target organizers over alleged selective outrage. Broadcaster reports highlighted these concurrent events. The Citizens Justice Party, founded by Abhijeet Dipke, specifically organized a gathering at Shivaji Park in Dadar, pressing three principal demands: the resignation of CEC Gyanesh Kumar, the freezing of the 2025 electoral list, and complete transparency in appointing future election chiefs. Large crowds, including youths and first-time voters from various parts of Maharashtra, gathered with national tricolours and placards despite the absence of formal police permission, under a heavy security presence. The organization plans further agitations across six other cities, culminating in an indefinite demonstration at Jantar Mantar in New Delhi by late October, following the earlier detentions in the capital.</p><p>What to watch: The planned indefinite demonstration in New Delhi by late October.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Covers the two disparate events well, though the transition between the pilot story and the political protests is abrupt.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.indiatoday.in/india/video/pm-modi-speaks-to-hero-flydubai-pilot-captain-smit-machhar-protests-on-october-2-ytvd-3008282-2026-10-02?utm_source=rss" rel="noopener">India Today</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
