     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-03 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Research Links Hurricane Exposure to Renter Eviction Risk</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Hurricanes</span> <span>#Renters</span> <span>#Eviction</span> <span>#Housing</span></div>
       <p>New research indicates that hurricanes can lead to the displacement of renters, even in instances where their homes sustain no physical damage. This finding comes from a study led by Rowan University urban planner Qian He, shedding light on a less-explored consequence of severe weather events on housing stability.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-03/research-links-hurricane-exposure-to-renter-eviction-risk.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The study highlights that the impact of hurricanes extends beyond structural damage, affecting renters' ability to remain in their homes. This displacement risk exists independently of whether the rental property itself is directly damaged by the storm.</p><p>The research suggests a broader understanding of hurricane aftermath is needed, considering the socio-economic vulnerabilities of renter populations.</p><p>What to watch: Further studies on non-physical hurricane impacts on housing.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is concise and accurately reflects the provided source summary.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-links-hurricane-exposure-higher-eviction.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
