     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-03 · editor 6/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Study Finds AI Usage Makes Corporate Earnings Calls Homogeneous</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A study published in the Academy of Management Proceedings reveals that companies increasingly sound alike when using AI tools for earnings presentations. Researchers, led by Binghamton University's Jujun Huang, found that prepared presentations show significant linguistic convergence, while live Q&amp;A sessions remain distinct due to their spontaneous nature.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-03/study-finds-ai-usage-makes-corporate-earnings-calls-homogene.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This stylistic similarity often results in negative market reactions, as investors perceive AI-assisted disclosures as boilerplate with less firm-specific information. While AI can improve clarity and efficiency, authors suggest it may smooth away the unique voice necessary for credible communication. Future research will examine whether these patterns persist in annual reports and sustainability disclosures.</p>
       
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ai-corporate-communication.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
