     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-03 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Supreme Court allows state National Song but bars penalties for objectors</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Supreme Court</span> <span>#National Song</span> <span>#Law</span> <span>#India</span></div>
       <p>The Supreme Court ruled that while a state may officially designate a National Song, it cannot impose penal action on individuals who conscientiously object to singing it.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-03/supreme-court-allows-state-national-song-but-bars-penalties-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The bench sought a response from the Union government on a petition filed by renowned Carnatic vocalist and author T.M. Krishna, who challenged the Prevention of Insults to National Honour (Amendment) Act, 2026.</p><p>The petition questions the act’s provisions that could criminalise dissent over national symbols, prompting the court to clarify the limits of state power.</p><p>What to watch: the Union government’s reply to the Supreme Court on the 2026 amendment.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately summarizes the court's action based on the provided source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/sc-says-state-can-set-national-song-but-cannot-impose-conscientious-objectors-to-penal-action/article71494664.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
