       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · News18 India · 2026-10-03 · editor 6/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>UAE Probes Flydubai Cockpit Attack for Possible Terror Link</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Flydubai</span> <span>#Cockpit Attack</span> <span>#UAE Probe</span> <span>#Aviation Security</span></div>
       <p>The United Arab Emirates has initiated an investigation into an incident aboard Flydubai flight FZ1073, which was forced to divert to Saudi Arabia following an attack in the cockpit. The flight, traveling from Dubai to Tel Aviv, landed safely at Tabuk Airport on Wednesday after an altercation in the flight deck involving 174 passengers, including 27 children.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-03/uae-probes-flydubai-cockpit-attack-for-possible-terror-link.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Indian pilot Captain Smit Machchhar sustained serious injuries after being attacked by his co-pilot, an individual believed to be an Omani national. Machchhar is reported to have fought back, a key action in preventing the situation from escalating further.</p><p>Investigations are ongoing, with authorities raising questions about a possible terror link and whether the incident involved external direction, according to News18 India. The serious cockpit altercation has also prompted broader concerns regarding aviation security and the safety of international air travel, a topic discussed by Captain Byron Bailey in an exclusive conversation with The 5 PM Brief.</p><p>What to watch: Further details on the investigation's findings into the terror link.</p>
       
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/videos/india/flydubai-cockpit-horror-uae-orders-probe-into-possible-terror-link-prior-planning-news18-10364758.html" rel="noopener">News18 India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
